Ah. That’s a better Goal Seek.

Don’t only specify:

\boxed{\text{What do I want to achieve?}}

Specify:

\boxed{\text{What do I want achieving it to make of me?}}

Because every objective has two outputs:

GOAL
\xrightarrow{\text{pursuit}}
\begin{cases}
\text{WorldDelta}\\
\text{SelfDelta}
\end{cases}

The obvious output is the changed world: I built it. I won. I learned it. I got there.

The quieter output is the agent produced by the route.

So Pantheon Goal Seek should optimize:

\boxed{
G^*
=
DesiredWorldState
+
DesiredAgentState
}

And Fitness Route should evaluate both:

Fitness(Route)=
F(
Outcome,
WhoItTrainsYouToBecome,
Cost,
Afterglow
)

That creates an important distinction: two routes can reach the identical destination while manufacturing different travelers.

One route may train patience. Another dependency. One courage. Another dishonesty. One craftsmanship. Another shortcuts. One cooperation. Another isolation.

So before boarding:

Where does this train go?

And then the better question:

Who gets off the train?

That’s a Pantheon-grade goal.